---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PinGenerator from '@/components/tools/security/PinGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pin-generator">
  <PinGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to generate a secure PIN</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Choose the length. Four digits for a bank card, six for a phone, more for
        anything where an attacker could try codes without a lockout.
      </li>
      <li>
        Leave the weak-pattern filter on. It removes the codes that appear at the
        top of every leaked PIN dataset, along with runs, repeats, palindromes, years and dates.
      </li>
      <li>
        Generate, and read the numbers. The panel shows how many codes remain after
        filtering and what that is worth in bits, so the cost of the filter is visible rather than
        assumed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Memorise it before you leave the page. Nothing is stored anywhere. Say it
        out loud a few times, or split it into two pairs and rehearse those.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How much protection does a PIN give?</h2>
    <p>
      Very little on its own, and that is fine: a PIN is never meant to withstand an unlimited
      attack. It works because something else limits the attempts: three tries before a cash machine
      swallows the card, ten before a phone wipes itself, a lockout timer that doubles after each
      failure. Remove that limit and even a twelve-digit PIN is weak.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Digits</th>
          <th>Possible codes</th>
          <th>Entropy</th>
          <th>3 guesses allowed</th>
          <th>Offline, 100 G/s</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>4</td><td>10,000</td><td>13.3 bits</td><td>1 chance in 3,333</td><td>instantly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td>100,000</td><td>16.6 bits</td><td>1 chance in 33,333</td><td>instantly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>19.9 bits</td><td>1 chance in 333,333</td><td>instantly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8</td><td>100,000,000</td><td>26.6 bits</td><td>1 chance in 33 million</td><td>instantly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10</td><td>10 billion</td><td>33.2 bits</td><td>1 chance in 3.3 billion</td><td>under a second</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12</td><td>1 trillion</td><td>39.9 bits</td><td>1 chance in 333 billion</td><td>5 seconds</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The last column is why a PIN should never be the only thing protecting data that can be copied
      off a device. If the storage is encrypted with a key derived from the PIN alone, and an
      attacker can take an image of the disk, they are not limited to three guesses any more.
    </p>

    <h2>The patterns this tool refuses to produce</h2>
    <p>
      Studies of leaked PIN datasets keep finding the same thing: roughly a quarter of all four-digit
      PINs come from a list of about twenty codes, and <code>1234</code> alone accounts for around
      one in ten. Human choice concentrates in a tiny corner of a small space. The filter removes
      that corner.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rejected pattern</th>
          <th>Examples</th>
          <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>One digit repeated</td>
          <td>0000, 1111, 8888</td>
          <td>Ten codes in total, and always tried first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Straight run</td>
          <td>1234, 4321, 6789</td>
          <td>A keypad walk in either direction. Attackers try it second.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Repeated block</td>
          <td>1212, 123123</td>
          <td>The attacker guesses the block, then the length.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Palindrome</td>
          <td>1221, 45654</td>
          <td>Memorable, and only a tenth of the space of a random code.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Year</td>
          <td>1987, 2024</td>
          <td>1900 to 2099 is 200 of the 10,000 four-digit codes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Day and month</td>
          <td>0312, 1203</td>
          <td>About 700 codes, and a stolen wallet usually contains the date of birth.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Six and eight-digit dates</td>
          <td>031287, 03121987</td>
          <td>How most people pick longer codes; an eight-digit birthday has under 40,000 options.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Does filtering make the PIN less random?</h3>
    <p>
      No, but a naive filter does. If you generated a code and then "fixed" it by changing digits
      until it passed, the result would be biased. This PIN generator discards a rejected code and
      draws a new one. Every code that
      survives is exactly as likely as every other, so the distribution stays uniform over a
      slightly smaller set. The entropy figure in the tool is computed from the size of that set,
      exactly for short PINs and by sampling for long ones.
    </p>

    <h2>Practical advice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Never use a date connected to you. Not your birthday, not your child's, not
        your wedding anniversary. A thief who has your wallet has your ID.
      </li>
      <li>
        Do not reuse a card PIN as a phone passcode. Someone who shoulder-surfs one
        gets the other.
      </li>
      <li>
        Mind the smudges. A greasy touchscreen shows which digits are involved,
        which cuts a four-digit search from 10,000 to a few dozen. Longer codes and the occasional
        deliberate wrong swipe both help.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prefer six digits to four wherever the device offers it,</strong> and a full
        alphanumeric passcode on anything that holds your email, because your email is the reset
        channel for all other accounts.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you must write it down, disguise it. A PIN hidden as a phone number in
        your contacts is far safer than the same PIN memorised badly and therefore replaced with
        1234.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
